/* Mock paper 1. t: 's' = single best answer (o options, a = index), 'v' = very short answer (a = accepted answers, m = model answer, req = keyword groups that must all appear).
   s = topic code (see TOPICS in mock-exam.js). e = explanation shown after marking. */
window.MOCK_PAPERS = window.MOCK_PAPERS || [];
window.MOCK_PAPERS.push({id:'p1',title:'Mock paper 1',sub:'Applied knowledge across all Year 1 systems',qs:[
/* ---- upper limb ---- */
{t:'s',s:'ul',q:'A 24-year-old rugby player has an anterior dislocation of the right shoulder reduced in the emergency department. Afterwards he cannot feel a light touch over the lower half of the deltoid, and shoulder abduction is weak. Which nerve has been injured?',
 o:['Suprascapular nerve','Axillary nerve','Musculocutaneous nerve','Radial nerve','Long thoracic nerve'],a:1,
 e:'The axillary nerve (C5–6) winds around the surgical neck of the humerus through the quadrangular space, right below the joint capsule, so it is stretched when the humeral head dislocates anteroinferiorly. It supplies deltoid and teres minor and, via the superior lateral cutaneous nerve of the arm, the "regimental badge" skin. Suprascapular injury weakens initiation of abduction and lateral rotation but causes no skin loss.'},
{t:'s',s:'ul',q:'A 35-year-old man has a mid-shaft fracture of the humerus. He has wrist drop and numbness over the dorsum of the first web space, but he can still extend his elbow strongly. What is the most likely site of nerve injury?',
 o:['Posterior cord of the brachial plexus','Radial nerve in the axilla','Radial nerve in the radial (spiral) groove','Posterior interosseous nerve in the supinator','C7 nerve root'],a:2,
 e:'The radial nerve lies in the radial groove on the back of the mid-shaft. Branches to the long and lateral heads of triceps leave above the groove, so elbow extension is spared. A posterior interosseous lesion would not cause sensory loss (the superficial radial branch is spared) and would leave wrist extension largely intact (ECRL is supplied before the split). An axillary or posterior cord lesion would weaken triceps too.'},
{t:'s',s:'ul',q:'A 52-year-old woman has tingling in her thumb, index and middle fingers that wakes her at night. There is wasting of the thenar eminence, but sensation over the thenar skin is normal. Why is sensation over the thenar eminence preserved?',
 o:['Thenar skin is supplied by the superficial branch of the radial nerve','The recurrent motor branch of the median nerve carries the sensory fibres','The palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve leaves above the flexor retinaculum and passes superficial to it','Thenar skin is supplied by the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm only','Sensory fibres are more resistant to compression than motor fibres'],a:2,
 e:'In carpal tunnel syndrome the median nerve is compressed under the flexor retinaculum. Its palmar cutaneous branch arises 3–5 cm proximal to the wrist and travels superficial to the retinaculum, so central palm and thenar skin keep normal sensation. Sensory loss over the thenar eminence suggests a more proximal median nerve lesion.'},
{t:'s',s:'ul',q:'A 19-year-old falls on an outstretched hand and has tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox. The initial X-ray is normal. Which feature of the injured bone explains why a missed fracture can lead to avascular necrosis?',
 o:['It has no periosteum','Its blood supply enters distally, so the proximal pole depends on vessels crossing the fracture','It is supplied only by the ulnar artery','It is entirely covered by articular cartilage and has no vessels','It is the last carpal bone to ossify'],a:1,
 e:'The scaphoid is supplied mainly by branches of the radial artery entering its distal pole and dorsal waist, so blood reaches the proximal pole retrogradely. A waist fracture can cut that supply, causing non-union and avascular necrosis of the proximal pole. This is why suspected scaphoid fractures are immobilised and re-imaged (or MRI) even with a normal first X-ray.'},
{t:'s',s:'ul',q:'A man cuts the medial side of his wrist on broken glass. He develops clawing of the ring and little fingers that is more marked than in a patient with an ulnar nerve injury at the elbow. What explains this difference?',
 o:['The median nerve is also cut at the wrist','Flexor digitorum profundus to the ring and little fingers still works and pulls the fingers into flexion','The lumbricals to the ring and little fingers are supplied by the median nerve','Extensor digitorum is paralysed in wrist lesions','Flexor carpi ulnaris is spared in elbow lesions'],a:1,
 e:'The "ulnar paradox": in a distal (wrist) lesion the ulnar-supplied medial half of FDP, innervated in the forearm, still works. With the 3rd and 4th lumbricals and interossei paralysed, the MCP joints hyperextend and the unopposed FDP flexes the IP joints, so clawing is worse. A proximal lesion also paralyses medial FDP, so the claw is milder.'},
{t:'s',s:'ul',q:'A 7-year-old has a displaced supracondylar fracture of the humerus. After reduction he cannot make an "OK" sign: the tip of the thumb and index finger stay straight. Sensation in the hand is normal. Which nerve is injured?',
 o:['Anterior interosseous nerve','Ulnar nerve','Radial nerve','Posterior interosseous nerve','Recurrent branch of the median nerve'],a:0,
 e:'The anterior interosseous nerve (branch of the median nerve) supplies flexor pollicis longus, the lateral half of FDP (index and middle fingers) and pronator quadratus. It has no cutaneous branch, so sensation is normal. It is the nerve most often injured in extension-type supracondylar fractures; the brachial artery is also at risk.'},
{t:'v',s:'ul',q:'During axillary node clearance for breast cancer, a nerve running on the deep surface of latissimus dorsi with the subscapular vessels is cut. Afterwards she finds it hard to pull herself up (extending and adducting the arm). Name the nerve.',
 a:['thoracodorsal nerve','thoracodorsal','nerve to latissimus dorsi','thoracodorsal n'],m:'Thoracodorsal nerve',
 e:'The thoracodorsal nerve (C6–8, from the posterior cord) runs with the thoracodorsal artery to supply latissimus dorsi, which extends, adducts and medially rotates the arm. The long thoracic nerve (serratus anterior, winging) is the other nerve at risk in axillary surgery.'},
{t:'v',s:'ul',q:'The lift-off (Gerber) test, in which the patient lifts the back of the hand off the lower back, tests which rotator cuff muscle?',
 a:['subscapularis','subscapularis muscle'],m:'Subscapularis',
 e:'Subscapularis is the only rotator cuff muscle that medially rotates the humerus. Supraspinatus initiates abduction, and infraspinatus and teres minor laterally rotate.'},
{t:'v',s:'ul',q:'A baby delivered by breech with the arm pulled above the head has a claw hand and, on the same side, a droopy eyelid and small pupil. Which spinal nerve root injury explains the eye signs?',
 a:['t1','t1 root','t1 nerve root','first thoracic','first thoracic root'],m:'T1',
 e:'Klumpke\'s palsy injures the C8–T1 roots (lower trunk). Preganglionic sympathetic fibres to the head leave the cord via the T1 ventral root and white ramus, so a T1 avulsion causes ipsilateral Horner\'s syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis).'},
/* ---- lower limb ---- */
{t:'s',s:'ll',q:'An 82-year-old woman falls and has a shortened, externally rotated left leg. X-ray shows a displaced intracapsular fracture of the femoral neck. Damage to which vessels makes avascular necrosis of the femoral head likely?',
 o:['Retinacular branches of the medial circumflex femoral artery','Artery of the ligamentum teres from the obturator artery','First perforating branch of the profunda femoris artery','Superior gluteal artery','Descending branch of the lateral circumflex femoral artery'],a:0,
 e:'In adults the femoral head is supplied mostly by retinacular arteries from the medial circumflex femoral artery, which run up the neck under the capsule and are torn by displaced intracapsular fractures. The artery of the ligamentum teres contributes little after childhood. This is why displaced intracapsular fractures are usually treated with arthroplasty rather than fixation.'},
{t:'s',s:'ll',q:'A 40-year-old has foot drop and numbness over the dorsum of the foot after a tight below-knee cast. Which examination finding would best distinguish an L5 radiculopathy from a common fibular nerve palsy?',
 o:['Weak ankle eversion','Weak ankle inversion','Absent ankle jerk','Numbness on the dorsum of the foot','Weak extension of the great toe'],a:1,
 e:'Inversion is done mainly by tibialis posterior (tibial nerve, L4–5), so it is weak in an L5 root lesion but preserved in a common fibular nerve palsy. Eversion, toe extension and dorsal foot sensation are lost in both. The ankle jerk is S1 and is normal in both. Hip abduction (gluteus medius, L5) is another discriminator.'},
{t:'s',s:'ll',q:'After a hip replacement through a lateral approach, a patient stands on her left leg and the right side of her pelvis drops. Which nerve has most likely been damaged?',
 o:['Right superior gluteal nerve','Left inferior gluteal nerve','Left superior gluteal nerve','Right femoral nerve','Left obturator nerve'],a:2,
 e:'Gluteus medius and minimus (superior gluteal nerve, L4–S1) on the stance side hold the pelvis level. If the left abductors are weak, the pelvis drops on the opposite (right) unsupported side: positive Trendelenburg sign on the left. The superior gluteal nerve runs between gluteus medius and minimus and is at risk in lateral approaches to the hip.'},
{t:'s',s:'ll',q:'A footballer twists his knee and hears a pop. Which feature of the history best suggests an anterior cruciate ligament rupture rather than an isolated meniscal tear?',
 o:['Knee locking in flexion','Swelling the next morning','Tenderness over the medial joint line','Large tense swelling within 1–2 hours','Pain on deep squatting'],a:3,
 e:'The ACL has a good blood supply, so rupture causes a haemarthrosis within an hour or two. The inner meniscus is avascular, so isolated meniscal tears swell slowly over 12–24 hours as an effusion. Locking and joint-line tenderness point to the meniscus. Lachman\'s test is the most sensitive examination for ACL rupture.'},
{t:'s',s:'ll',q:'A 60-year-old woman has a tender irreducible lump in the groin. Which description fits a femoral hernia?',
 o:['Emerges through the deep inguinal ring, above and medial to the pubic tubercle','Passes through Hesselbach\'s triangle, above the inguinal ligament','Passes through the femoral canal, below and lateral to the pubic tubercle','Passes through the obturator foramen, deep to pectineus','Lies lateral to the femoral artery in the femoral triangle'],a:2,
 e:'Femoral hernias pass through the femoral canal, medial to the femoral vein (NAVY: nerve, artery, vein, Y-fronts/empty space), and appear below and lateral to the pubic tubercle. Inguinal hernias appear above and medial to it. The femoral ring is narrow and unyielding, so femoral hernias strangulate easily and are more common in women.'},
{t:'s',s:'ll',q:'Six hours after intramedullary nailing of a tibial fracture, a patient has severe pain made worse by passive plantarflexion of the toes. Which early sensory finding would point to the anterior compartment of the leg?',
 o:['Numbness of the sole','Numbness over the lateral malleolus','Numbness over the medial calf','Numbness of the first dorsal web space','Numbness of the heel'],a:3,
 e:'The anterior compartment contains the deep fibular nerve, which supplies skin of the first web space. Raised compartment pressure compresses it early. Passive plantarflexion stretches the anterior compartment muscles, which is why it hurts. Compartment syndrome needs urgent fasciotomy; pulses are usually still present.'},
{t:'v',s:'ll',q:'The saphenofemoral junction, where the great saphenous vein joins the femoral vein, lies about 3–4 cm inferolateral to which palpable bony landmark?',
 a:['pubic tubercle','the pubic tubercle','pubic tubercule'],m:'Pubic tubercle',
 e:'The great saphenous vein passes through the saphenous opening in the fascia lata about 3–4 cm inferolateral to the pubic tubercle to join the femoral vein. This is the landmark for varicose vein surgery and for examining the junction for incompetence.'},
{t:'v',s:'ll',q:'A posterolateral disc prolapse at L4/5 causes weakness of great toe extension and numbness over the dorsum of the foot. Which nerve root is compressed?',
 a:['l5','l5 root','l5 nerve root','fifth lumbar'],m:'L5',
 e:'A posterolateral lumbar disc prolapse usually compresses the traversing root, which exits one level below (at L4/5 that is L5). L5 supplies extensor hallucis longus and the skin of the dorsum of the foot. A far-lateral prolapse would hit the exiting L4 root.'},
{t:'v',s:'ll',q:'Which muscle "unlocks" the fully extended knee at the start of flexion by laterally rotating the femur on the fixed tibia?',
 a:['popliteus','popliteus muscle'],m:'Popliteus',
 e:'At full extension the femur medially rotates on the tibia (screw-home), locking the knee. Popliteus (tibial nerve) reverses this rotation to unlock it so flexion can begin.'},
/* ---- general MSK ---- */
{t:'s',s:'msk',q:'Denosumab is given to a postmenopausal woman with osteoporosis. It reduces bone resorption by blocking which interaction?',
 o:['PTH binding to its receptor on osteoblasts','RANKL binding to RANK on osteoclast precursors','Calcitonin binding to osteoclasts','Osteoprotegerin binding to RANKL','Sclerostin binding to LRP5/6'],a:1,
 e:'Osteoblasts and osteocytes express RANKL, which binds RANK on osteoclast precursors to drive their formation and activity. Osteoprotegerin is the body\'s own decoy receptor. Denosumab is a monoclonal antibody against RANKL that mimics osteoprotegerin.'},
{t:'s',s:'msk',q:'In skeletal muscle, what directly causes calcium to be released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum after an action potential travels down a T-tubule?',
 o:['Calcium entering through L-type channels binds ryanodine receptors (calcium-induced calcium release)','IP₃ binds receptors on the sarcoplasmic reticulum','Dihydropyridine receptors mechanically open ryanodine receptors','Acetylcholine binds receptors on the sarcoplasmic reticulum','Sodium entry reverses the Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchanger'],a:2,
 e:'In skeletal muscle the dihydropyridine receptor acts as a voltage sensor physically linked to RyR1, so depolarisation opens RyR1 without needing extracellular Ca²⁺. Calcium-induced calcium release is the mechanism in cardiac muscle; IP₃ matters in smooth muscle.'},
{t:'s',s:'msk',q:'A 30-year-old woman has drooping eyelids and double vision that get worse through the day and improve after rest. What is the underlying mechanism?',
 o:['Antibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels','Demyelination of peripheral nerves','Antibodies against postsynaptic nicotinic acetylcholine receptors','Cleavage of SNARE proteins','Loss of dystrophin from the sarcolemma'],a:2,
 e:'Myasthenia gravis is caused by autoantibodies against postsynaptic nicotinic ACh receptors, giving fatigable weakness that typically affects eye muscles first. Lambert–Eaton syndrome (presynaptic Ca²⁺ channel antibodies) improves with repeated effort; SNARE cleavage is botulism; dystrophin loss is Duchenne muscular dystrophy.'},
{t:'s',s:'msk',q:'Which of these is a primary cartilaginous joint (synchondrosis)?',
 o:['Pubic symphysis','Intervertebral disc','Epiphyseal growth plate','Sagittal suture','Distal tibiofibular joint'],a:2,
 e:'Primary cartilaginous joints are joined by hyaline cartilage and usually ossify: the epiphyseal growth plate and the first sternocostal joint. Secondary cartilaginous joints (symphyses) use fibrocartilage and lie in the midline: pubic symphysis, intervertebral discs. Sutures and the distal tibiofibular joint (syndesmosis) are fibrous.'},
{t:'v',s:'msk',q:'Which molecule must bind to the myosin head to release it from actin at the end of a power stroke? (Its absence after death causes rigor mortis.)',
 a:['atp','adenosine triphosphate'],m:'ATP',
 e:'Binding of ATP detaches myosin from actin; hydrolysis of ATP then re-cocks the head. Without ATP after death, cross-bridges stay locked (rigor mortis).'},
{t:'v',s:'msk',q:'What type of cartilage forms the menisci of the knee and the annulus fibrosus of the intervertebral disc?',
 a:['fibrocartilage','fibro cartilage','fibrocartilaginous','white fibrocartilage'],m:'Fibrocartilage',
 e:'Fibrocartilage contains dense type I collagen bundles as well as type II collagen, giving tensile strength. It is found in menisci, the annulus fibrosus, the pubic symphysis and the labra.'},
/* ---- nervous ---- */
{t:'s',s:'nerv',q:'A man is stabbed in the back and has a hemisection of the right side of his spinal cord at T10. Which finding would you expect?',
 o:['Loss of vibration sense in the left leg','Loss of pain and temperature sensation on the left from about T11–T12 downwards','Weakness of the left leg','Loss of pain and temperature sensation on the right below T10','Flaccid weakness of both arms'],a:1,
 e:'Brown-Séquard syndrome: the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tract are ipsilateral below the cord, so there is right-sided loss of vibration and proprioception and right-sided UMN weakness. Spinothalamic fibres cross within 1–2 segments of entry, so pain and temperature are lost on the left starting a level or two below the lesion.'},
{t:'s',s:'nerv',q:'A 70-year-old right-handed man suddenly develops weakness of the right face and arm (leg less affected) and can understand speech but produces only halting, effortful words. Which artery is most likely occluded?',
 o:['Left anterior cerebral artery','Right middle cerebral artery','Left middle cerebral artery','Left posterior cerebral artery','Basilar artery'],a:2,
 e:'The left (dominant) MCA supplies the lateral motor cortex for face and arm and Broca\'s area in the inferior frontal gyrus, giving expressive aphasia. ACA occlusion affects the leg more; PCA occlusion causes contralateral homonymous hemianopia.'},
{t:'s',s:'nerv',q:'Which examination finding indicates a lower motor neuron lesion?',
 o:['Brisk reflexes','Upgoing plantar response','Clasp-knife spasticity','Fasciculations','Sustained ankle clonus'],a:3,
 e:'Fasciculations (visible twitches of motor units) with wasting, low tone and reduced reflexes are lower motor neuron signs. The other options are upper motor neuron signs.'},
{t:'s',s:'nerv',q:'A child has congenital stenosis of the cerebral aqueduct. Which parts of the ventricular system will be dilated on a scan?',
 o:['Fourth ventricle only','Lateral ventricles only','Lateral ventricles and third ventricle','Third and fourth ventricles','All four ventricles and the subarachnoid space'],a:2,
 e:'CSF flows lateral ventricles → interventricular foramina → third ventricle → cerebral aqueduct → fourth ventricle → subarachnoid space. A block at the aqueduct dilates everything upstream (lateral and third ventricles) and leaves the fourth ventricle normal: obstructive (non-communicating) hydrocephalus.'},
{t:'s',s:'nerv',q:'During a lumbar puncture at L3/4, which layer is the last one the needle passes through before CSF is obtained?',
 o:['Ligamentum flavum','Dura mater','Pia mater','Arachnoid mater','Posterior longitudinal ligament'],a:3,
 e:'Order: skin, subcutaneous tissue, supraspinous ligament, interspinous ligament, ligamentum flavum ("give" felt), epidural space, dura mater, arachnoid mater, then the subarachnoid space with CSF. Pia mater coats the roots and is not pierced.'},
{t:'s',s:'nerv',q:'A patient takes an overdose of an antimuscarinic drug. Which sympathetic function is most directly reduced, contributing to hyperthermia?',
 o:['Pupil dilation','Sweating','Heart rate increase','Renin release','Lipolysis'],a:1,
 e:'Eccrine sweat glands receive sympathetic postganglionic fibres that release acetylcholine onto muscarinic receptors. Blocking them stops sweating ("dry as a bone, hot as a hare"). Pupil dilation, tachycardia and renin release are adrenergic and are not blocked.'},
{t:'s',s:'nerv',q:'What is mainly responsible for the absolute refractory period of a neuronal action potential?',
 o:['Activity of the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase','Open voltage-gated K⁺ channels','Inactivation of voltage-gated Na⁺ channels','Influx of chloride ions','Closure of leak K⁺ channels'],a:2,
 e:'After opening, voltage-gated Na⁺ channels enter an inactivated state and cannot reopen until the membrane repolarises, so no second action potential is possible however strong the stimulus. During the relative refractory period some Na⁺ channels have recovered but K⁺ conductance is still high.'},
{t:'s',s:'nerv',q:'A 55-year-old woman has a sudden severe headache. Her right eyelid droops, the eye looks down and out, and the right pupil is dilated and unreactive. What is the most likely cause?',
 o:['Diabetic microvascular infarction of the oculomotor nerve','Posterior communicating artery aneurysm compressing the oculomotor nerve','Lesion of the right abducens nerve','Right-sided Horner\'s syndrome','Myasthenia gravis'],a:1,
 e:'Parasympathetic pupillomotor fibres run on the surface of CN III, so external compression (classically a posterior communicating artery aneurysm) affects the pupil early. Diabetic microvascular palsy affects the core of the nerve and usually spares the pupil. Horner\'s gives a small pupil and mild ptosis.'},
{t:'s',s:'nerv',q:'In Guillain–Barré syndrome, which cell type is the main target of the immune attack?',
 o:['Oligodendrocyte','Astrocyte','Microglia','Schwann cell','Ependymal cell'],a:3,
 e:'Guillain–Barré is an acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy of the peripheral nervous system, where myelin is made by Schwann cells. Multiple sclerosis targets CNS myelin made by oligodendrocytes.'},
{t:'v',s:'nerv',q:'In a right-handed person, in which lobe of the brain is Broca\'s area found?',
 a:['frontal','frontal lobe','left frontal lobe','left frontal','dominant frontal lobe','inferior frontal gyrus'],m:'Frontal lobe (left inferior frontal gyrus)',
 e:'Broca\'s area is in the inferior frontal gyrus of the dominant (usually left) frontal lobe. Damage causes expressive (non-fluent) aphasia. Wernicke\'s area, for comprehension, is in the superior temporal gyrus.'},
{t:'v',s:'nerv',q:'Which cranial nerve carries the parasympathetic fibres that stimulate secretion from the parotid gland?',
 a:['glossopharyngeal','glossopharyngeal nerve','cn ix','cn9','ix','9','ninth cranial nerve'],m:'Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)',
 e:'Preganglionic fibres leave with CN IX, travel in the lesser petrosal nerve to the otic ganglion, and postganglionic fibres reach the parotid via the auriculotemporal nerve. The facial nerve passes through the parotid but does not supply it.'},
{t:'v',s:'nerv',q:'Degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in which midbrain structure causes Parkinson\'s disease?',
 a:['substantia nigra','substantia nigra pars compacta','pars compacta','the substantia nigra'],m:'Substantia nigra (pars compacta)',
 e:'Loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta reduces dopamine in the striatum, causing bradykinesia, rigidity and resting tremor.'},
/* ---- cardiovascular ---- */
{t:'s',s:'cvs',q:'A 62-year-old has ST elevation in leads II, III and aVF and develops complete heart block. Which artery is most likely occluded?',
 o:['Left anterior descending artery','Circumflex artery','Right coronary artery','Left main stem','Diagonal branch of the LAD'],a:2,
 e:'Leads II, III and aVF look at the inferior wall, usually supplied by the right coronary artery via the posterior descending artery in right-dominant hearts. The AV nodal artery comes from the RCA in about 80–90% of people, so inferior MI often causes AV block.'},
{t:'s',s:'cvs',q:'An 80-year-old has an ejection systolic murmur radiating to both carotids and a slow-rising pulse. Where is this murmur best heard?',
 o:['Right 2nd intercostal space at the sternal edge','Left 2nd intercostal space at the sternal edge','Left 4th intercostal space at the sternal edge','5th intercostal space in the mid-clavicular line','Left mid-axillary line'],a:0,
 e:'This is aortic stenosis. The aortic area is the right 2nd intercostal space by the sternum, and the murmur radiates along the direction of flow into the carotids. The pulmonary area is the left 2nd space; the mitral area is the apex.'},
{t:'s',s:'cvs',q:'A dehydrated patient is given a 500 mL fluid bolus and their stroke volume rises. What is the main mechanism?',
 o:['Increased sympathetic stimulation of the ventricles','Increased end-diastolic stretch of myocardial fibres','Reduced afterload from arteriolar dilatation','Increased heart rate from the Bainbridge reflex','Increased coronary blood flow'],a:1,
 e:'The Frank–Starling mechanism: more venous return raises end-diastolic volume, stretching sarcomeres towards optimal actin–myosin overlap and increasing troponin Ca²⁺ sensitivity, so the heart ejects more. This is intrinsic and does not need nerves or hormones.'},
{t:'s',s:'cvs',q:'In which nerve do the afferent fibres from the carotid sinus baroreceptors travel?',
 o:['Vagus nerve','Glossopharyngeal nerve','Hypoglossal nerve','Phrenic nerve','Sympathetic chain'],a:1,
 e:'The carotid sinus nerve is a branch of CN IX. Aortic arch baroreceptors send their afferents in the vagus (CN X). Both terminate in the nucleus tractus solitarius in the medulla.'},
{t:'s',s:'cvs',q:'Which part of the conduction system has the slowest conduction velocity, and why does this matter?',
 o:['Purkinje fibres: so that the ventricles contract from base to apex','AV node: so that the atria finish emptying before the ventricles contract','SA node: so that the heart rate stays below 100 per minute','Bundle of His: so that the two ventricles contract together','Internodal tracts: so that the left atrium contracts first'],a:1,
 e:'The AV node conducts slowly (small cells, few gap junctions, Ca²⁺-dependent action potentials), producing most of the PR interval. This gives atrial systole time to top up the ventricles. Purkinje fibres are the fastest-conducting tissue in the heart.'},
{t:'s',s:'cvs',q:'During which phase of the cardiac cycle are all four valves closed while ventricular pressure is rising?',
 o:['Atrial systole','Isovolumetric contraction','Rapid ejection','Isovolumetric relaxation','Rapid ventricular filling'],a:1,
 e:'Isovolumetric contraction runs from mitral and tricuspid closure (S1) to the opening of the aortic and pulmonary valves. Volume is constant while pressure rises steeply. Isovolumetric relaxation also has all valves closed, but pressure is falling.'},
{t:'s',s:'cvs',q:'A 68-year-old has a hoarse voice. A CT scan shows an aneurysm of the aortic arch. Which nerve is most likely compressed?',
 o:['Right recurrent laryngeal nerve','Left phrenic nerve','Left recurrent laryngeal nerve','External laryngeal nerve','Left vagus nerve above the arch'],a:2,
 e:'The left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the arch of the aorta just lateral to the ligamentum arteriosum before ascending to the larynx, so arch aneurysms, hilar lymph nodes or tumours there can paralyse the left vocal fold. The right recurrent nerve hooks under the right subclavian artery.'},
{t:'s',s:'cvs',q:'Which vessels contribute most to total peripheral resistance?',
 o:['Elastic arteries','Muscular arteries','Arterioles','Capillaries','Venules'],a:2,
 e:'Arterioles have a thick smooth muscle wall relative to their lumen, and resistance varies with 1/radius⁴, so small changes in arteriolar tone change resistance greatly. Capillaries are narrower individually but there are so many in parallel that their total resistance is lower.'},
{t:'s',s:'cvs',q:'A 25-year-old with nephrotic syndrome has pitting oedema of both legs. What is the main mechanism?',
 o:['Raised capillary hydrostatic pressure','Reduced plasma oncotic pressure','Increased capillary permeability','Lymphatic obstruction','Increased interstitial hydrostatic pressure'],a:1,
 e:'Heavy albuminuria lowers plasma albumin, reducing the oncotic pressure that draws fluid back into capillaries, so fluid accumulates in the interstitium (Starling forces). Raised hydrostatic pressure is the main cause in heart failure; increased permeability in inflammation and sepsis.'},
{t:'v',s:'cvs',q:'Name the large vein in the posterior atrioventricular groove that returns most of the heart\'s own venous blood to the right atrium.',
 a:['coronary sinus','the coronary sinus'],m:'Coronary sinus',
 e:'The great, middle and small cardiac veins drain into the coronary sinus, which opens into the right atrium near the tricuspid valve. Small anterior cardiac veins and Thebesian veins drain directly into the chambers.'},
{t:'v',s:'cvs',q:'What is the name of the depression in the interatrial septum that is the remnant of the fetal foramen ovale?',
 a:['fossa ovalis','the fossa ovalis'],m:'Fossa ovalis',
 e:'At birth, left atrial pressure rises above right, pushing the septum primum against the septum secundum and closing the foramen ovale. The resulting depression on the right atrial side is the fossa ovalis. If it fails to seal, a patent foramen ovale remains.'},
{t:'v',s:'cvs',q:'Influx of which ion causes the rapid upstroke (depolarisation) of the sinoatrial node action potential?',
 a:['calcium','ca','ca2+','ca2','calcium ions','calcium ion','ca++'],m:'Calcium (through L-type channels)',
 e:'SA node cells lack fast Na⁺ channels. After the slow "funny" current (If) and T-type Ca²⁺ current bring the cell to threshold, L-type Ca²⁺ channels open and produce the upstroke. This is why calcium-channel blockers such as verapamil slow the heart rate.'},
/* ---- respiratory ---- */
{t:'s',s:'resp',q:'A 3-year-old inhales a peanut while sitting upright. Where is it most likely to lodge?',
 o:['Left main bronchus','Right main bronchus','Larynx at the vocal folds','Lingula','Left lower lobe bronchus'],a:1,
 e:'The right main bronchus is wider, shorter and more vertical than the left, so inhaled objects usually enter it (and then the right lower lobe when upright). In children the difference in angle is less marked, but right-sided lodgement is still more common.'},
{t:'s',s:'resp',q:'When inserting a chest drain, why should the needle and drain pass just above the upper border of a rib?',
 o:['To avoid the internal thoracic artery','The pleura is thinnest there','The intercostal vein, artery and nerve run in the costal groove under the rib above','To avoid the long thoracic nerve','The intercostal muscles are absent there'],a:2,
 e:'The intercostal neurovascular bundle (vein, artery, nerve from top to bottom) runs in the costal groove along the inferior border of each rib, between the internal and innermost intercostal muscles. Going over the top of the rib below avoids it.'},
{t:'s',s:'resp',q:'A baby born at 28 weeks develops grunting, recession and a "ground-glass" chest X-ray. Deficiency of a substance made by which cell is responsible?',
 o:['Type I pneumocytes','Type II pneumocytes','Alveolar macrophages','Club (Clara) cells','Goblet cells'],a:1,
 e:'Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome is caused by too little surfactant, made by type II pneumocytes from about 24 weeks and in adequate amounts from about 34 weeks. Without it, surface tension collapses small alveoli. Antenatal corticosteroids speed surfactant production.'},
{t:'s',s:'resp',q:'During heavy exercise, which change in the working muscle helps haemoglobin unload more oxygen?',
 o:['Fall in 2,3-BPG','Rise in pH','Rise in hydrogen ion concentration','Fall in temperature','Rise in carbon monoxide'],a:2,
 e:'More H⁺ (lower pH) and more CO₂ shift the oxygen–haemoglobin dissociation curve to the right (Bohr effect), as do higher temperature and 2,3-BPG, so Hb releases more O₂ at a given PO₂. The other options shift the curve left or reduce O₂ delivery.'},
{t:'s',s:'resp',q:'A 64-year-old smoker has spirometry: FEV₁ 1.2 L (40% predicted), FVC 3.0 L (79% predicted). How should this be interpreted?',
 o:['Normal spirometry','Restrictive pattern','Obstructive pattern','Mixed pattern with normal ratio','Upper airway obstruction only'],a:2,
 e:'FEV₁/FVC = 1.2/3.0 = 0.40, well below 0.7, with a relatively preserved FVC: an obstructive pattern such as COPD. In a restrictive pattern both FEV₁ and FVC fall and the ratio is normal or high.'},
{t:'s',s:'resp',q:'Which receptors are mainly responsible for increasing ventilation when arterial PO₂ falls?',
 o:['Central chemoreceptors in the medulla','Peripheral chemoreceptors in the carotid bodies','Pulmonary stretch receptors','Juxtacapillary (J) receptors','Baroreceptors in the carotid sinus'],a:1,
 e:'Only peripheral chemoreceptors (mainly the carotid bodies, afferents in CN IX) respond to hypoxaemia, especially when PaO₂ falls below about 8 kPa. Central chemoreceptors respond to the H⁺ produced when CO₂ diffuses into the CSF and do not sense oxygen.'},
{t:'s',s:'resp',q:'Inflammation under the central part of the diaphragm causes pain felt at the tip of the shoulder. Which nerve carries the pain, and from which spinal segments?',
 o:['Intercostal nerves, T7–T11','Vagus nerve','Phrenic nerve, C3–C5','Suprascapular nerve, C5–C6','Greater splanchnic nerve, T5–T9'],a:2,
 e:'The central diaphragm and its pleura and peritoneum are supplied by the phrenic nerve (C3–5). The skin over the shoulder tip is supplied by C4 (supraclavicular nerves), so the pain is referred there. The peripheral diaphragm is supplied by the lower intercostal nerves.'},
{t:'s',s:'resp',q:'Which situation produces a shunt (perfusion without ventilation, V/Q ≈ 0)?',
 o:['Pulmonary embolism','Lobar pneumonia with consolidation','Emphysema with alveolar wall destruction','Anatomical dead space','Standing upright (apical alveoli)'],a:1,
 e:'In consolidation the alveoli are full of exudate but still perfused, so blood passes without being oxygenated. Shunted hypoxaemia does not correct well with extra oxygen. Pulmonary embolism produces dead space (ventilation without perfusion).'},
{t:'v',s:'resp',q:'At which vertebral level (in the textbook supine position) does the trachea bifurcate at the carina?',
 a:['t4','t5','t4/5','t4-5','t4-t5','t4/t5','t4 t5','t4 5','sternal angle','t4t5'],m:'T4/T5 (level of the sternal angle)',
 e:'The carina lies at the level of the sternal angle and the T4/5 intervertebral disc. In a standing person on deep inspiration it may descend to about T6.'},
{t:'v',s:'resp',q:'Name the only laryngeal cartilage that forms a complete ring around the airway.',
 a:['cricoid','cricoid cartilage','the cricoid'],m:'Cricoid cartilage',
 e:'The cricoid cartilage (C6) is signet-ring shaped and the only complete ring. Pressure on it (cricoid pressure) was traditionally used to compress the oesophagus during rapid-sequence induction. The tracheal rings are C-shaped.'},
/* ---- GI ---- */
{t:'s',s:'gi',q:'A 78-year-old with atrial fibrillation has sudden severe abdominal pain out of proportion to the examination. CT shows an embolus blocking the superior mesenteric artery near its origin. Which part of the gut is at risk?',
 o:['Stomach and first part of the duodenum','Jejunum, ileum, caecum, ascending colon and proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon','Descending colon, sigmoid colon and rectum','Liver, gallbladder and pancreas','Oesophagus and stomach'],a:1,
 e:'The SMA supplies the midgut: from the major duodenal papilla to two-thirds of the way along the transverse colon. Acute mesenteric ischaemia classically causes pain out of proportion to signs and a raised lactate. The foregut is supplied by the coeliac trunk; the hindgut by the IMA.'},
{t:'s',s:'gi',q:'Why is the pain of early appendicitis felt around the umbilicus?',
 o:['The appendix lies behind the umbilicus','Visceral afferents from the midgut enter the spinal cord at about T10','The parietal peritoneum over the umbilicus is inflamed first','The vagus nerve refers pain to the umbilicus','The ilioinguinal nerve supplies the appendix'],a:1,
 e:'Visceral pain fibres from midgut structures run with sympathetic nerves to about T10, the dermatome of the umbilicus, so the brain localises the pain there. When the inflamed appendix irritates the parietal peritoneum (somatic nerves), pain moves to the right iliac fossa.'},
{t:'s',s:'gi',q:'A 50-year-old with autoimmune gastritis develops macrocytic anaemia and peripheral neuropathy. Loss of which gastric cell explains this?',
 o:['Chief cells','G cells','Parietal cells','D cells','Enterochromaffin-like cells'],a:2,
 e:'Parietal cells secrete HCl and intrinsic factor. Autoimmune destruction of parietal cells (or antibodies to intrinsic factor) prevents B12 absorption in the terminal ileum: pernicious anaemia, with macrocytic anaemia and subacute combined degeneration of the cord.'},
{t:'s',s:'gi',q:'A 70-year-old man has painless jaundice, dark urine, pale stools and itching. His gallbladder is palpable. Where is the obstruction most likely to be?',
 o:['Cystic duct, caused by a gallstone','Head of the pancreas compressing the common bile duct','Hepatocytes (intrahepatic cholestasis)','Common hepatic duct above the cystic duct junction','Haemolysis with excess bilirubin production'],a:1,
 e:'Courvoisier\'s law: in painless jaundice, a palpable gallbladder makes gallstones unlikely (stone disease scars and shrinks the gallbladder) and suggests a malignant obstruction of the distal bile duct, such as cancer of the pancreatic head. A block above the cystic duct would not distend the gallbladder.'},
{t:'s',s:'gi',q:'A man with alcoholic cirrhosis vomits blood from oesophageal varices. Which portal tributary forms one side of the portosystemic anastomosis involved?',
 o:['Splenic vein','Superior rectal vein','Left gastric vein','Paraumbilical vein','Inferior mesenteric vein'],a:2,
 e:'At the lower oesophagus, the left gastric vein (portal) anastomoses with oesophageal veins draining into the azygos system (systemic). In portal hypertension these dilate into varices. Paraumbilical veins cause caput medusae; superior rectal veins cause rectal varices.'},
{t:'s',s:'gi',q:'A woman has her terminal ileum resected for Crohn\'s disease. Which deficiency is she most at risk of in the long term?',
 o:['Iron','Folate','Vitamin B12','Calcium','Vitamin C'],a:2,
 e:'B12–intrinsic factor complexes and bile salts are absorbed in the terminal ileum. Iron and calcium are mostly absorbed in the duodenum, folate in the jejunum. She also risks bile-acid diarrhoea and gallstones.'},
{t:'s',s:'gi',q:'After a paracetamol overdose, which region of the liver lobule shows the first and worst necrosis?',
 o:['Zone 1, around the portal triads','Zone 2, mid-lobule','Zone 3, around the central vein','Bile ducts in the portal tracts','Kupffer cells in all zones equally'],a:2,
 e:'Zone 3 (centrilobular) hepatocytes receive the least oxygenated blood and contain the most cytochrome P450 (CYP2E1), which turns paracetamol into the toxic NAPQI. They are damaged first by both ischaemia and paracetamol.'},
{t:'s',s:'gi',q:'Which enzyme first activates trypsinogen in the duodenum?',
 o:['Pepsin','Enteropeptidase (enterokinase)','Chymotrypsin','Carboxypeptidase','Colipase'],a:1,
 e:'Enteropeptidase on the duodenal brush border cleaves trypsinogen to trypsin, which then activates the other pancreatic zymogens (and more trypsinogen). Keeping enzymes inactive until they reach the duodenum protects the pancreas; activation inside the gland causes pancreatitis.'},
{t:'s',s:'gi',q:'Which of these structures is intraperitoneal, suspended by a mesentery?',
 o:['Second part of the duodenum','Ascending colon','Head of the pancreas','Transverse colon','Left kidney'],a:3,
 e:'The transverse colon hangs on the transverse mesocolon. The others are retroperitoneal (SAD PUCKER: suprarenals, aorta/IVC, duodenum 2nd–4th parts, pancreas except tail, ureters, colon ascending and descending, kidneys, oesophagus, rectum).'},
{t:'v',s:'gi',q:'Name the sphincter at the hepatopancreatic ampulla that controls the flow of bile and pancreatic juice into the duodenum.',
 a:['sphincter of oddi','oddi','oddis sphincter','sphincter of oddi muscle','hepatopancreatic sphincter'],m:'Sphincter of Oddi',
 e:'The sphincter of Oddi surrounds the ampulla of Vater at the major duodenal papilla. CCK relaxes it while contracting the gallbladder.'},
{t:'v',s:'gi',q:'Which hormone, released by duodenal I cells in response to fat and protein, makes the gallbladder contract?',
 a:['cholecystokinin','cck','cholecystokinin cck'],m:'Cholecystokinin (CCK)',
 e:'CCK contracts the gallbladder, relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, stimulates pancreatic enzyme secretion and slows gastric emptying. This is why fatty meals trigger biliary colic.'},
/* ---- renal ---- */
{t:'s',s:'renal',q:'A patient with type 2 diabetes is started on dapagliflozin and develops glycosuria. Where in the nephron does the drug act?',
 o:['Glomerulus','Proximal convoluted tubule','Thick ascending limb','Distal convoluted tubule','Collecting duct'],a:1,
 e:'Dapagliflozin inhibits SGLT2 in the early proximal tubule, which normally reabsorbs about 90% of filtered glucose. Blocking it causes glucose (and some sodium) to be lost in the urine.'},
{t:'s',s:'renal',q:'Furosemide causes a large diuresis. Which transporter does it block?',
 o:['SGLT2 in the proximal tubule','NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb','NCC in the distal convoluted tubule','ENaC in the collecting duct','Aquaporin-2 in the collecting duct'],a:1,
 e:'Loop diuretics block the Na⁺/K⁺/2Cl⁻ co-transporter in the thick ascending limb, which reabsorbs about 25% of filtered sodium and builds the medullary concentration gradient. That is why they are so powerful. They also increase Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ loss.'},
{t:'s',s:'renal',q:'After a head injury, a patient passes large volumes of dilute urine and his plasma sodium rises. The deficient hormone is synthesised where?',
 o:['Posterior pituitary','Anterior pituitary','Supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus','Zona glomerulosa of the adrenal cortex','Juxtaglomerular cells'],a:2,
 e:'This is cranial diabetes insipidus from lack of ADH. ADH is synthesised by neurons in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei, transported down their axons and released from the posterior pituitary, which stores but does not make it.'},
{t:'s',s:'renal',q:'A 30-year-old has hypertension caused by narrowing of one renal artery. Which cells secrete the enzyme that starts the hormonal cascade raising his blood pressure?',
 o:['Macula densa cells','Juxtaglomerular (granular) cells in the afferent arteriole','Podocytes','Principal cells of the collecting duct','Mesangial cells inside the glomerulus'],a:1,
 e:'Low perfusion pressure at the afferent arteriole makes juxtaglomerular cells release renin, which converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I; ACE then forms angiotensin II. The macula densa senses tubular NaCl and signals to the JG cells but does not secrete renin itself.'},
{t:'s',s:'renal',q:'A man with severe loin-to-groin pain has a 5 mm ureteric stone. Where is the stone most likely to impact?',
 o:['Renal calyx','Pelviureteric junction','Where the ureter crosses the psoas','Vesicoureteric junction','Bladder neck'],a:3,
 e:'The ureter has three narrowings: the pelviureteric junction, the pelvic brim (crossing the iliac vessels) and the vesicoureteric junction, which is the narrowest, where most stones stick.'},
{t:'s',s:'renal',q:'An elderly man with bilateral renal artery stenosis is started on ramipril, and his creatinine rises sharply. Why?',
 o:['Ramipril constricts the afferent arteriole','Ramipril is directly toxic to the proximal tubule','Loss of angiotensin II–mediated efferent arteriolar constriction lowers glomerular filtration pressure','Ramipril blocks renin release','Ramipril increases sodium reabsorption'],a:2,
 e:'When renal perfusion pressure is low, GFR depends on angiotensin II constricting the efferent arteriole to keep glomerular capillary pressure up. ACE inhibitors remove this, so GFR falls. NSAIDs do the same from the other side by blocking prostaglandin-mediated afferent dilatation.'},
{t:'s',s:'renal',q:'An arterial blood gas shows pH 7.31, PaCO₂ 3.8 kPa, HCO₃⁻ 14 mmol/L. What is the best interpretation?',
 o:['Respiratory acidosis with metabolic compensation','Metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation','Metabolic alkalosis','Respiratory alkalosis','Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis'],a:1,
 e:'Low pH with low bicarbonate is a metabolic acidosis. The low PaCO₂ is the expected respiratory compensation (hyperventilation); Winter\'s formula predicts PaCO₂ ≈ 1.5 × 14 + 8 = 29 mmHg ≈ 3.9 kPa, which matches. If PaCO₂ were high, it would be a mixed acidosis.'},
{t:'v',s:'renal',q:'Name the smooth triangular area of the bladder wall between the two ureteric openings and the internal urethral orifice.',
 a:['trigone','the trigone','trigone of bladder','vesical trigone','bladder trigone'],m:'Trigone',
 e:'The trigone has a smooth surface (unlike the rugae elsewhere) because its mucosa is firmly attached to the underlying muscle. It derives from the mesonephric ducts and is a common site for bladder tumours and infection.'},
{t:'v',s:'renal',q:'A man with chronic kidney disease has a normocytic anaemia. Deficiency of which kidney hormone is the main cause?',
 a:['erythropoietin','epo','erythropoeitin','erythropoetin'],m:'Erythropoietin (EPO)',
 e:'Peritubular interstitial cells in the renal cortex make erythropoietin in response to hypoxia. In CKD production falls, causing a normocytic anaemia that can be treated with recombinant EPO (after correcting iron).'},
/* ---- endocrine ---- */
{t:'s',s:'endo',q:'A 32-year-old woman has weight loss, tremor, a diffuse goitre and protruding eyes. TSH is suppressed and free T4 is high. What is the mechanism?',
 o:['Antibodies that block the TSH receptor','Antibodies that stimulate the TSH receptor','Antibodies against thyroid peroxidase destroying follicles','A TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma','Excess iodine intake'],a:1,
 e:'Graves\' disease is caused by IgG antibodies that stimulate the TSH receptor, driving hormone production and gland growth. TSH is suppressed by negative feedback. Eye disease (exophthalmos) is specific to Graves\'. Anti-TPO antibodies are typical of Hashimoto\'s.'},
{t:'s',s:'endo',q:'A 45-year-old has resistant hypertension and low potassium. Plasma renin is low and aldosterone high. Where is the tumour most likely to be?',
 o:['Adrenal medulla','Zona fasciculata','Zona glomerulosa','Zona reticularis','Anterior pituitary'],a:2,
 e:'Primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn\'s syndrome) from an aldosterone-secreting adenoma of the zona glomerulosa. High aldosterone causes Na⁺ retention, hypertension and K⁺ loss; renin is suppressed. Secondary hyperaldosteronism (e.g. renal artery stenosis) has high renin.'},
{t:'s',s:'endo',q:'Why must prednisolone taken for many months not be stopped suddenly?',
 o:['It causes rebound hyperglycaemia','Suppression of CRH and ACTH has left the adrenal cortex atrophied and unable to make cortisol','It causes rebound excess of aldosterone','It damages the adrenal medulla','It permanently destroys the anterior pituitary'],a:1,
 e:'Exogenous glucocorticoids exert negative feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary, so ACTH falls and the zona fasciculata atrophies. Sudden withdrawal leaves no cortisol: an adrenal crisis (hypotension, hypoglycaemia, collapse). Doses are tapered to let the axis recover.'},
{t:'s',s:'endo',q:'Insulin increases glucose uptake by moving GLUT4 transporters to the cell membrane in which tissue?',
 o:['Liver','Brain','Red blood cells','Skeletal muscle','Pancreatic β cells'],a:3,
 e:'GLUT4 is the insulin-responsive transporter in skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle and adipose tissue. The liver and β cells use GLUT2; brain and red cells use GLUT1/GLUT3, which do not depend on insulin.'},
{t:'s',s:'endo',q:'The day after a total thyroidectomy, a woman has tingling around her mouth and spasm of her hands when a blood pressure cuff is inflated. What is the cause?',
 o:['Damage to the recurrent laryngeal nerve','Removal or ischaemia of the parathyroid glands','Loss of calcitonin from the thyroid','Hypothyroidism','Hypomagnesaemia from anaesthetic drugs'],a:1,
 e:'The parathyroids lie on the back of the thyroid and depend on the inferior thyroid arteries; they can be removed or devascularised. Low PTH causes hypocalcaemia: perioral paraesthesia, Trousseau\'s sign (carpal spasm with a cuff) and Chvostek\'s sign. Loss of calcitonin has little effect in humans.'},
{t:'s',s:'endo',q:'A 40-year-old has headaches and has started walking into door frames. He has lost the outer half of the visual field in each eye. What is the most likely cause?',
 o:['Lesion of the right optic nerve','Pituitary adenoma compressing the optic chiasm','Lesion of the left optic tract','Occipital lobe infarct','Glaucoma'],a:1,
 e:'The optic chiasm sits just above the pituitary. Fibres from the nasal half of each retina (seeing the temporal fields) cross in the chiasm, so pressure from below gives a bitemporal hemianopia. Optic tract and occipital lesions give homonymous defects.'},
{t:'v',s:'endo',q:'Which posterior pituitary hormone stimulates uterine contraction in labour and milk ejection during breastfeeding?',
 a:['oxytocin'],m:'Oxytocin',
 e:'Oxytocin is made in the paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei. Cervical stretch (Ferguson reflex) and suckling trigger positive-feedback release.'},
{t:'v',s:'endo',q:'Through which vascular system do hypothalamic releasing hormones reach the anterior pituitary?',
 a:['hypophyseal portal system','hypophyseal portal','portal system','hypothalamo hypophyseal portal system','hypothalamic hypophyseal portal system','pituitary portal system','hypophyseal portal veins','portal veins','hypothalamic pituitary portal system'],m:'Hypophyseal (hypothalamo-hypophyseal) portal system',
 e:'Releasing hormones enter capillaries in the median eminence and are carried by portal veins down the stalk to a second capillary bed in the anterior pituitary, at high concentration. The posterior pituitary is reached by axons instead.'},
/* ---- reproductive ---- */
{t:'s',s:'repro',q:'Which cell in the testis secretes inhibin B, which feeds back to suppress FSH?',
 o:['Leydig cell','Sertoli cell','Spermatogonium','Peritubular myoid cell','Epididymal epithelial cell'],a:1,
 e:'Sertoli cells, stimulated by FSH, support spermatogenesis, form the blood–testis barrier, make androgen-binding protein and secrete inhibin B. Leydig cells make testosterone under LH.'},
{t:'s',s:'repro',q:'What triggers the mid-cycle LH surge that leads to ovulation?',
 o:['A fall in progesterone from the corpus luteum','A sustained high level of oestradiol switching to positive feedback','A rise in inhibin from the dominant follicle','A fall in GnRH pulse frequency','hCG from the endometrium'],a:1,
 e:'Through most of the cycle oestradiol inhibits LH and FSH. When the dominant follicle keeps oestradiol high (roughly above 200 pg/mL for about 2 days), feedback becomes positive, causing a surge of LH (and FSH). Ovulation follows about 24–36 hours later.'},
{t:'s',s:'repro',q:'A 28-year-old man has a testicular germ cell tumour. To which lymph nodes does it spread first?',
 o:['Superficial inguinal nodes','Deep inguinal nodes','Internal iliac nodes','Para-aortic (lumbar) nodes','Axillary nodes'],a:3,
 e:'The testes develop on the posterior abdominal wall and descend, taking their vessels and lymphatics with them. Lymph follows the testicular vessels to para-aortic nodes at about L1–L2. Scrotal skin drains to superficial inguinal nodes.'},
{t:'s',s:'repro',q:'Where does fertilisation normally take place?',
 o:['Uterine cavity','Isthmus of the uterine tube','Ampulla of the uterine tube','Infundibulum at the fimbriae','Surface of the ovary'],a:2,
 e:'Fertilisation usually occurs in the ampulla, the widest and longest part of the uterine tube. The conceptus then travels to the uterus and implants around 6–7 days after fertilisation. The ampulla is also the most common site of ectopic pregnancy.'},
{t:'v',s:'repro',q:'Which hormone, produced by the syncytiotrophoblast, keeps the corpus luteum alive in early pregnancy and is detected by pregnancy tests?',
 a:['hcg','human chorionic gonadotropin','human chorionic gonadotrophin','beta hcg','bhcg','chorionic gonadotrophin','chorionic gonadotropin'],m:'Human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG)',
 e:'hCG acts on LH receptors of the corpus luteum, maintaining progesterone production until the placenta takes over at about 8–10 weeks.'},
/* ---- immune & lymphatic ---- */
{t:'s',s:'imm',q:'A baby with DiGeorge syndrome has an absent thymus. Which region of a lymph node would be most depleted of cells?',
 o:['Primary follicles','Germinal centres','Paracortex','Medullary cords','Subcapsular sinus'],a:2,
 e:'T cells mature in the thymus and populate the paracortex of lymph nodes (and periarteriolar sheaths in the spleen). Follicles and germinal centres are B-cell areas; medullary cords hold plasma cells.'},
{t:'s',s:'imm',q:'A 20-year-old has a splenectomy after a road traffic collision. Against which group of organisms is he now at greatest risk?',
 o:['Intracellular viruses such as herpes viruses','Fungi such as Candida','Encapsulated bacteria such as Streptococcus pneumoniae','Gram-negative bacteria of the gut','Mycobacteria'],a:2,
 e:'The spleen clears opsonised bacteria and makes IgM against polysaccharide capsules (marginal zone B cells). Without it, encapsulated organisms (S. pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae type b, Neisseria meningitidis) can cause overwhelming sepsis. Vaccination and antibiotic prophylaxis are recommended.'},
{t:'s',s:'imm',q:'A woman has a breast cancer in the upper inner (medial) quadrant. Besides the axillary nodes, which nodes are most likely to receive its lymph?',
 o:['Supraclavicular nodes on the opposite side','Parasternal (internal thoracic) nodes','Inguinal nodes','Deep cervical nodes','Para-aortic nodes'],a:1,
 e:'About 75% of breast lymph goes to the axillary nodes, but medial quadrants also drain to the parasternal nodes along the internal thoracic vessels. Lower quadrants can drain towards the abdominal wall.'},
{t:'s',s:'imm',q:'A newborn is protected against measles for several months by maternal antibodies. Which antibody class crossed the placenta?',
 o:['IgA','IgM','IgG','IgE','IgD'],a:2,
 e:'IgG is actively transported across the placenta by the neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn). IgM is too large; IgA reaches the baby through breast milk and protects the gut.'},
{t:'v',s:'imm',q:'Into which venous junction does the thoracic duct usually drain?',
 a:['left venous angle','left subclavian and internal jugular','junction of left subclavian and left internal jugular veins','left subclavian vein','left internal jugular and subclavian','left brachiocephalic vein','junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins'],
 req:['left','subclavian|jugular|venous angle|brachiocephalic'],m:'Left venous angle (junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins)',
 e:'The thoracic duct starts at the cisterna chyli (L1/2), passes through the aortic hiatus and drains into the left venous angle. It carries lymph from everywhere except the right upper quadrant of the body, which drains via the right lymphatic duct.'},
/* ---- blood ---- */
{t:'s',s:'blood',q:'Two days after starting warfarin, a patient\'s INR has risen even though full anticoagulation has not yet been reached. Loss of which clotting factor explains the early INR rise?',
 o:['Factor II (prothrombin)','Factor VII','Factor IX','Factor X','Factor VIII'],a:1,
 e:'Warfarin blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase, reducing functional factors II, VII, IX and X (and proteins C and S). Factor VII has the shortest half-life (about 6 hours), so it falls first and the PT/INR rises early. Prothrombin\'s half-life is about 60 hours, so true anticoagulation takes days. Factor VIII is not vitamin K–dependent.'},
{t:'s',s:'blood',q:'Why does fetal haemoglobin have a higher affinity for oxygen than adult haemoglobin?',
 o:['It has four α chains','Its γ chains bind 2,3-BPG less strongly','It contains ferric (Fe³⁺) iron','It is found at a higher pH','It lacks haem groups in two chains'],a:1,
 e:'HbF (α₂γ₂) binds 2,3-BPG poorly, so its dissociation curve lies to the left of HbA. This allows fetal blood to take oxygen from maternal blood across the placenta.'},
{t:'v',s:'blood',q:'A patient is blood group O RhD-negative. Which ABO antibodies are in their plasma?',
 a:['anti-a and anti-b','anti a and anti b','anti-a anti-b','both anti-a and anti-b','a and b antibodies','anti-a and b','antia and antib'],req:['antia|aandb|aantibod|antiaand','antib|bantibod|aandb'],m:'Anti-A and anti-B',
 e:'Group O red cells carry neither A nor B antigen, so the plasma contains naturally occurring IgM anti-A and anti-B. This is why group O patients can only receive group O red cells, while their red cells can be given to anyone (universal red cell donor).'},
/* ---- skin ---- */
{t:'s',s:'skin',q:'A man is burned by a house fire. One area on his thigh is white, leathery, does not blanch and is painless to a pinprick. What does this indicate?',
 o:['Superficial (epidermal) burn','Superficial partial-thickness burn','Full-thickness burn with destruction of dermal nerve endings','Burn with spinal cord damage','Deep partial-thickness burn with intact nerve endings'],a:2,
 e:'A full-thickness burn destroys the whole dermis, including its blood vessels (no blanching) and nerve endings (painless). It cannot heal from adnexal epithelium and needs excision and grafting. Partial-thickness burns are painful.'}
]});
